<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Network Security Configuration for BasicBrowser.

    This configuration controls which CAs and connections this app trusts.

    IMPORTANT LIMITATIONS FOR WEBVIEW:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Android's Network Security Config (NSC) only accepts CA certificates
    embedded as build-time resources (res/raw/). Our development CA is
    generated at runtime (first app launch), so it CANNOT be referenced
    here.

    WebView trust for the runtime-generated development CA is handled
    programmatically via a selective onReceivedSslError handler in the
    WebViewClient. That handler validates certificates against our
    specific dev CA — it does NOT blindly accept all certificates.

    See: DevCaSslErrorHandler.kt

    WHAT THIS FILE DOES:
    - Trusts only the system CA store for normal HTTPS (standard behavior)
    - Permits cleartext HTTP traffic globally (<base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">)

    SECURITY IMPLICATIONS OF CLEARTEXT PERMISSION:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Setting cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" allows Android WebView and
    networking clients in this app to make unencrypted HTTP connections.
    Because this application functions as a web browser and security-testing
    client intended to browse external websites, local development servers,
    and intentionally vulnerable testbeds:
    1. Cleartext HTTP is required for WebView to navigate to HTTP URLs
       (e.g., http://neverssl.com, http://10.0.2.2:8000). Otherwise,
       Android's Chromium stack blocks navigation with
       net::ERR_CLEARTEXT_NOT_PERMITTED prior to routing traffic to the
       local proxy (127.0.0.1:8080).
    2. Sensitive data transmitted over plain HTTP is not encrypted over
       the wire to the target server, as is inherent to the HTTP protocol.
    3. For all HTTPS connections, strict system trust anchors (<certificates src="system" />)
       and full TLS certificate-chain and RFC 2818/6125 hostname verification
       remain fully enforced. No permissive trust managers are introduced.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <!--
        Base configuration:
        - System and User CAs for HTTPS validation:
          Adding <certificates src="user" /> allows Android Chromium / WebView to trust
          user-installed CA certificates (such as our exported Development CA or proxy
          intercept certificates) across all top-level navigations, iframes, and cross-origin
          subresources (images, scripts, stylesheets) without throwing net_error -202.
          SECURITY NOTE: Trusting user-installed certificates applies to all user-installed
          CAs on the device, not exclusively this app's CA. Upstream HTTPS connections made
          by TlsInterceptor continue to validate real server certificates and enforce
          strict RFC 2818/6125 hostname verification.
        - Cleartext permitted to support browser HTTP navigation and local proxy routing
    -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

</network-security-config>
